      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "With the queue system this could even be incorporated into it as a \"parking\" instance so to speak.\n\nIf you join the queue to go into an instance, you could be presented with a choice to be placed into the \"parking\" instance to wait for a spot to open (as an example).\n\nThe UI would just need some way to clearly state you're joining the parking instance while waiting for your spot in the intended instance to not confuse users.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2021-05-04T06:56:13.847Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "Currently when joining a instance that is full, the user gets a message stating that the instance is full then gets redirected to a friends only instance of the world.\nWith many large events happening in VRChat now, getting a full instance happens a lot.  And a lot of times the community would also have an overflow instance for everyone to join if they can't get in the main one. By having the ability to assign an overflow instance, instead of the user getting redirected to a empty friends only instance, when a instance is full, the user would get redirected to the overflow.\nOne method that comes to mind is to use the website. Probably the way to determine which instance is the overflow is to have submit field to input a world link or maybe instance ID for the overflow instance on the same page as \"Launch World\" and \"Invite Me\".\nSo if that field has had something submitted, when a user joins the main instance and it's full they get redirected to the instance that has been submitted instead of a empty friends only.\nI believe this would make things better for both the event hosts and the event attendees.  \nAnd to ensure no one does anything malicious this can only be done on non-public instances and if it has to be a world link the world ID in the link for the overflow must match the world ID of the main instance and both instances have to be owned by the same user.\nAnd there can be an extra checks that any inputted instance (overflow) does not have an overflow set to the original instance to prevent putting users in an infinite loop.",
